# Merge Policy

Merge is a separately authorized operation. A positive readiness assessment is
diagnostic evidence, never merge authorization.

## Required merge conditions

Before the merge write, collect the complete fixed-order reader chain for the
exact pull request and build one immutable `PullRequestReadinessEvidence`
snapshot. Verify all of the following against the snapshot's bound head and
base identities:

- The pull request is open and not a Draft.
- No unresolved merge conflicts remain.
- No open, blocking `REQUEST_CHANGES` review remains.
- All repository-required approvals are satisfied.
- All checks required by current branch protection or applicable rulesets have
  completed successfully. Optional checks must not be treated as required, and
  missing or unavailable policy evidence is not a pass.
- Exactly one issue is linked, with the relationship supported by explicit
  issue or pull-request evidence; OR the unique-link requirement is waived
  by an exact exception recorded in `MergeReadiness.issue_coverage` for
  the exact repository, pull request, and current head SHA, with complete
  waiver evidence (reason, waiver source, and recorded justification).
- No scope blocker, unexpected change, or clarification remains unresolved.

The merge is blocked when any condition is failed, stale, unavailable, or
ambiguous. Recheck branch protection, applicable rulesets, required reviews,
required checks, mergeability, and base-branch state while building the
snapshot. A new snapshot is required after every rebase or push; no individual
source may be reused in isolation. The final S03 snapshot is the only current
policy source for required checks, approval thresholds, change requests, and
review-thread dispositions. A diagnostic `MergeReadiness` summary cannot
replace or override that snapshot.

## Deterministic pre-merge gate

Immediately before the GitHub merge write, `merge-pull-request` MUST write a
current local version-3 `PreMergeGate` containing the exact pull-request
identity, expected head and base SHAs, final live preflight, selected strategy,
branch-deletion effect, version-3 `MergeReadiness`, its complete version-1
`PullRequestReadinessEvidence`, and explicit merge authorization. The
host-specific `pre-merge` Hook MUST fail closed unless the local gate and
embedded snapshot agree for:

- open, non-Draft status;
- active reviews and change requests;
- open blocking review threads;
- required approvals and their current policy source;
- every required status check;
- mergeability and absence of conflicts;
- current base branch SHA;
- exactly one linked issue relationship OR a waived unique-link condition;
  and
- the exact user or repository-policy merge authorization and selected strategy.

Every supported merge command MUST also carry an exact full expected-head
compare-and-set: `--match-head-commit <sha>` for `gh pr merge`, or `sha=<sha>`
for the supported merge API payload. Missing or mismatched protection is a
pre-write denial.

The Hook does not execute `gh`, GraphQL, or any other live GitHub acquisition;
policy interpretation belongs to the reader Skills and the snapshot builder.

The Hook must stop when any identity, readiness, authorization, review,
thread, approval, check, conflict, base, or issue condition changes or is
unavailable. It may report the missing evidence and a safe next step, but it
must never resolve a blocker, rerun a check, mutate GitHub, rewrite the gate,
or perform the merge.

## Repository strategy and authorization

Use only a merge strategy currently allowed by the repository and GitHub.
When more than one strategy is allowed, the exact user authorization or an
applicable repository policy must select one; never infer a default. The exact
authorization must identify the repository, pull request, head and base
revisions, selected strategy, commit metadata, and any branch-deletion effect.

Rebase and merge are independent operations with independent authorizations.
Authorization for one never authorizes the other. After an authorized rebase,
invalidate all prior source handoffs, readiness snapshots, and validation
evidence, then revalidate the new head's identity, history, diff scope, tests,
checks, reviews, issue linkage, conflicts, and merge readiness before
requesting or performing a merge.

## Post-merge boundary

Verify the merge state, merge commit, target branch, and actual strategy before
starting any follow-up workflow. Issue-closure verification and issue closure
are separate steps and may begin only after a verified merge; when
`MergeReadiness.issue_coverage.status` is `waived`, issue-closure
verification is skipped. After
`verify-linked-issue-closure` returns `status: not-closed` with
`closure.expected: true`, `close-linked-issue` may perform the single exact
fallback close using the validated close-on-merge intent as its authorization
source. This exception does not authorize neutral `Refs` relationships,
additional issues, comments, or any other mutation. Branch deletion and local
or remote worktree cleanup are separate, independently authorized operations
and must never begin before that verification. A clear, scope-matched
target-repository `AGENTS.md` policy may supply those separate authorizations;
otherwise their owning workflows retain their user-approval gates.

The host-specific `post-merge` Hook is a read-only observer after an identified
GitHub merge command. It may inspect the current pull request, merge commit,
target-branch containment, expected issue closure, and local or remote cleanup
availability, then return a version-1 `PostMergeStatus` with open actions and
deviations. It must never close an issue, delete a branch, remove a worktree,
write hook state, or infer cleanup authorization. Every cleanup action remains
blocked until its owning workflow receives separate exact user or
repository-policy authorization.
